 {
  "part": 5,
  "title": "Combine queries & build keys",
  "html": "<div class=\"crumb\">Part 5 of 6</div>\n    <h1>Combine queries &amp; build keys</h1>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Merge = add columns (horizontal). Append = add rows (vertical).</p>\n\n    <h2>1. Merge queries — the join kinds</h2>\n    <div class=\"tblw\"><table class=\"tbl\">\n      <tr><th>Join kind</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Left Outer</b></td><td>All rows from the first table + matching rows from the second</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Right Outer</b></td><td>All rows from the second table + matching rows from the first</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Full Outer</b></td><td>All rows from both tables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Inner</b></td><td>Only rows that match in both tables</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Left Anti</b></td><td>Rows in the first table with <b>no</b> match in the second</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><b>Right Anti</b></td><td>Rows in the second table with <b>no</b> match in the first</td></tr>\n    </table></div>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Select the matching column(s) in <i>both</i> tables (Ctrl-click for composite keys).</li>\n      <li>The merge adds a column of type <b>Table</b> — click the expand icon to pick the columns you need.</li>\n      <li><b>Fuzzy matching</b> lets near-matches join (spelling differences).</li>\n      <li><b>Merge Queries as New</b> leaves the originals untouched.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>2. Append queries</h2>\n    <p>Stacks rows of tables with the same (or similar) columns. <b>Append Queries</b> changes the current query; <b>Append Queries as New</b> creates a new query. Columns are matched by <i>name</i>; missing ones become null.</p>\n\n    <h2>3. Reference vs Duplicate</h2>\n    <table class=\"tbl\">\n      <tr><th>Reference</th><th>Duplicate</th></tr>\n      <tr><td>New query starts from the <b>output</b> of the original. Changes to the original flow through.</td><td>Copy of all the steps. Independent afterwards.</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <p>Typical recipe to build a <b>dimension table</b> from a fact table: <i>Reference</i> the fact query → Remove other columns (keep the attributes) → <b>Remove Duplicates</b> on the key.</p>\n\n    <h2>4. Keys and star schema</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The “one” side of a relationship needs a unique, non-empty key (check with column distribution).</li>\n      <li>Create a key by merging columns (<code>Merge Columns</code>) or adding an <b>Index Column</b> (surrogate key).</li>\n      <li>Aim for a <b>star schema</b>: one narrow fact table surrounded by dimension tables.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note trap\"><b class=\"t\">Exam trap</b>“Keep all rows from table A and bring data from B” → <b>Left Outer</b> with A first. “Rows in A that do not exist in B” → <b>Left Anti</b>. Merge adds columns; Append adds rows.</div>\n    <div class=\"note\"><b class=\"t\">Key takeaways</b>\n      <ul class=\"check\">\n        <li>Know all six join kinds and which table must be first.</li>\n        <li>Append as New keeps the sources intact.</li>\n        <li>Reference for dependent queries, Duplicate for independent copies.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>"
 },
 {
  "part": 6,
  "title": "Performance, parameters & M",
  "html": "<div class=\"crumb\">Part 6 of 6</div>\n    <h1>Performance, parameters &amp; M</h1>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Make queries fast, reusable and ready to load.</p>\n\n    <h2>1. Query folding</h2>\n    <p>Query folding means Power Query translates your steps into <b>one native query</b> (for example SQL) that runs at the source, instead of pulling all data and transforming it locally.</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Usually folds: filter rows, remove / rename / choose columns, sort, group by, merge/append of the same source.</li>\n      <li>Often <b>breaks</b> folding: <b>Add Index Column</b>, custom functions, merges across different sources, <code>Table.Buffer</code>.</li>\n      <li>Verify: right-click a step → <b>View Native Query</b>. If it is greyed out, the step does <b>not</b> fold.</li>\n      <li>Best practice: put foldable steps (filters, column removal) <b>first</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>2. Parameters</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Home → Manage Parameters → New Parameter</b>: name, type, suggested values (Any value, List of values, Query), current value.</li>\n      <li>Use them for server / database names, file paths, filter values.</li>\n      <li>After publishing, edit them in the service: <b>Semantic model settings → Parameters</b> (no Desktop needed).</li>\n      <li><code>RangeStart</code> and <code>RangeEnd</code> (Date/Time type, exactly these names) enable <b>incremental refresh</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>3. Load options &amp; model size</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Uncheck <b>Enable load</b> on staging / helper queries so they aren't loaded into the model.</li>\n      <li><b>Include in report refresh</b> controls whether the query refreshes.</li>\n      <li>Remove unused columns, filter unneeded rows early, use proper types, avoid high-cardinality columns (split DateTime into Date + Time).</li>\n      <li>Finish with <b>Close &amp; Apply</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>4. Reading M</h2>\n<pre class=\"code\"><span class=\"k\">let</span>\n    Source  = Sql.Database(<span class=\"s\">\"srv-prod\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"Sales\"</span>),\n    Orders  = Source{[Schema=<span class=\"s\">\"dbo\"</span>, Item=<span class=\"s\">\"Orders\"</span>]}[Data],\n    Recent  = Table.SelectRows(Orders, <span class=\"k\">each</span> [OrderDate] &gt;= #date(2024,1,1)),\n    Slim    = Table.SelectColumns(Recent, {<span class=\"s\">\"OrderID\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"OrderDate\"</span>, <span class=\"s\">\"Amount\"</span>})\n<span class=\"k\">in</span>\n    Slim</pre>\n    <ul>\n      <li><code>let … in</code> — each line is a named <b>step</b>; the last one after <code>in</code> is the result. Applied Steps = these names.</li>\n      <li>M is <b>case-sensitive</b> (<code>Table.SelectRows</code> ≠ <code>table.selectrows</code>).</li>\n      <li><code>each</code> is shorthand for a function with the row as <code>_</code>.</li>\n      <li>M is for <b>loading / shaping</b>; DAX is for <b>calculating</b> in the model — they are not interchangeable.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"note trap\"><b class=\"t\">Exam trap</b>Disabling load doesn't delete a query — other queries can still reference it. And “Index column” in a SQL source stops folding for the steps after it.</div>\n    <div class=\"note\"><b class=\"t\">Key takeaways</b>\n      <ul class=\"check\">\n        <li>View Native Query enabled = step folds.</li>\n        <li>Parameters make Dev → Prod switching and incremental refresh possible.</li>\n        <li>Smaller model: fewer columns, fewer rows, no loading of staging queries.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>"
 }
